+## Deploying with Kamal
+
+The included `config/deploy.yml` is configured for one Linux server. Fizzy, all
+SQLite databases, uploaded files, and Solid Queue run in one application
+container. Kamal Proxy terminates TLS with a Let's Encrypt certificate, and the
+`fizzy_storage` volume keeps the database and uploads across deploys.
+
+In your fork, replace `fizzy.example.com` in the config with the hostname whose
+DNS record points to the server. Replace the `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` and `SMTP_*`
+values with the settings from your mail provider. The server needs Docker and
+SSH access, and ports 80 and 443 must be open. Change `builder.arch` to `arm64`
+if the server uses that architecture instead of `amd64`.
+
+Generate one Rails secret and VAPID key pair, then make the deployment secrets
+referenced by `.kamal/secrets` available to Kamal through your shell or secret
+manager:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails secret
+
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE="the-generated-value"
+export SMTP_USERNAME="your-smtp-username"
+export SMTP_PASSWORD="your-smtp-password"
+export VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY="your-vapid-public-key"
+export VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY="your-vapid-private-key"
+```
+
+Store the secret values securely and keep the same values for every deploy.
+VAPID keys can be generated as described in the
+[development guide](docs/development.md#web-push-notifications).
+
+After the hostname and mail settings are committed, run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+Kamal installs Docker when needed, pulls the image through its local registry,
+prepares the persistent SQLite database, and starts the app. Visit
+`https://your-host` to create the first account. Use `bin/kamal deploy` for
+subsequent deployments; the mounted `fizzy_storage` volume is reused.
+
+
